i'm larry goldstein  i'm a professor of cellular and  molecular medicine at uc san diego  and i have the great pleasure today to  interview my friend  colleague comrade in arms paul berg  nobel prize winner and also a recipient  of the public policy award from the  american society for cell biology  i'm going to ask paul some questions  about his life as a science policy  advocate  and public figure so paul thank you for  taking the time to join me today  thank you good so so let's start at the  beginning  you had a very illustrious career in  science  but something made you decide to  leave the lab at least part of the time  and engage in  policy debates what was it that brought  you out for the first time  well it certainly wasn't voluntary  it turned out that an experiment which  we had planned and were in the midst of  elicited a lot of concern amongst other  scientists  the experiment we had in progress was to  fuse a small  mini chromosome of containing lambda  lambda genes  and three genes of e coli the gal operon  and we fuse that with sv40 to make a  recombinant that contain  now this recombinant could propagate in  e coli  because it contained the parts on the  mini plasmid which allowed it to  propagate  e coli and it could also be propagated  and transfect  mammalian cells so it was kind of a dual  headed thing and what we wanted to do is  to be able to make mutations  in the sv40 genome in coli and then test  them  in mammalian cells and the other thing  we were going to test and see whether  the gal genes could be expressed in  mammals right so that was the idea of  the experiment  the point was that we were going to be  propagating  sv40 genes in e coli right right so the  concern was  coli is a natural inhabitant in humans  and the concern was  inadvertently could you in fact humans  with the  e coli you were propagating and could  that possibly lead to cancer right  that was the concern and depending upon  where you came down on the topic you  were either vociferous about it  yeah or you were just saying forget  about it it's not serious  okay but i soon after a lot of  consultations i said  became convinced that i could not  dismiss the risk and make  zero likelihood and so i thought well  we'll just put it on the shelf and so  the whole thing died down  yeah a year later or not  quite a year later janet mertz a  graduate student in my lab  discovered that eco r1 made cohesive  ends and she discovered that she could  take any two dnas and join them together  and so stan cohn and her boyer then  began to actually  make a lot of recombinants using e coli  plasmids right  and checking drug resistance and showing  that they can make chimeric dna  molecules  and they talked about it at a gordon  conference and that  elicited the same kind of response  amongst a small minority of the audience  who said oh my god you guys can really  make some dangerous things  so what happens is at that gordon  conference they got maxine singer  and dieter saul who were the co-chairs  to draft a letter  to the academy and to publish that  letter in science  calling attention to the great promise  that being able to make  new kinds of dna molecules uh but also  calling attention to possible  dangers and risks right and when the  president of the academy got this  letter he said what do i do with it and  maxine said to him  call paul berg he's thought about this  problem  right and so he asked me  would i put together a group of people  that could advise the academy on how to  respond to the concerns  that had been raised and  so i did i put together a small group  and we met  at mit dave baltimore dan nathan's  myself jim watson a few other  norton zinder people who would  understand  the nature of the problem we met for a  whole day as group  and in the end there was no advice that  we could provide we didn't know what to  do  so we said you know literally you could  pick up the phone and call everybody  of your scientific community who's  likely to use the technology and say  hey be careful yup but we couldn't do  that so we said well the next best thing  is why don't we just  publish a letter in science  calling attention to the promise to some  of the potential risks that have been  raised  and essentially  calling essentially asking the  the scientific community to put a short  hold  on three kinds of experiments we didn't  call for a moratorium  all this kind of work there was three  kinds of experiments that we  specifically  called attention to and in the letter we  proposed having an international  conference  some six or eight months later to  examine the risks in more detail  and come forward with some  recommendations i think the scientific  community got a lot of  good kudos from the public right saying  here's the problem  scientists call it attention you  actually got out of it  how to how to deal with this thing yeah  so  if you look back now you say i got into  public policy  through my own experiments yeah and just  being called on  to say what do we do yeah when somebody  raised the risk so  the initial so-called moratorium letter  which came out of the group  uh called for a meeting  to deal with it and so we convened i was  the chair of convening the  syllabar meeting and the organizing  committee was maxine dave baltor myself  zinder sydney brenner we had a person  from europe  and we were going to organize a meeting  of experts to try to assess  what was the magnitude of the risks what  was the likelihood of  them coming true and how could we  proceed  in any case okay so the syllabar meeting  was a major event 150  people huge representation from the  media which was probably one of the best  things we decided  there were 15 people from press and  newspapers magazines and so on  and in those three days we debated  what are the kinds of experiments you  want to do what are the possible risks  and we organized it very well so the  meeting was not all talking about risks  it was talking about science and that  was probably a saving grace to the  why the meeting succeeded because people  came prepared to talk about scientific  experiments  and they were balanced by discussion  there's a lot of interesting science  came out of the  syllabar meeting yeah one was the safe  bug  to engineer a microbe right which could  not escape from the lab  right okay so the crippled host the  crippled host right and the second one  was actually  so that was sydney brenner's idea it's a  good idea he brought that  to the meeting and and i think that was  turned people around a lot because  suddenly  we could answer the question that you  could devise the experiments in some way  which would markedly lessen the  likelihood of escape  so this organism wouldn't be able to  live outside the lab  okay so there were a number so right in  that meeting  you could see science taking over  people beginning with well we could do  this well and i think you and i would  agree  if you're going to have good science  policy it has to be  driven by good science right  misunderstanding is not going to get the  job done  the turning point of the meeting frankly  was the last night  when the lawyers had their session oh  and the ethicists yeah we'd invited a  group of lawyers and ethicists and did  the lawyers and ethicists sit through  the sciences  they participated in everything as did  the press yeah the press were free to  ask questions raise points  and in the drinking in the evenings they  milled around  and they were full members of the  of the meeting the lawyers pointed out  the legal problems right and challenged  jim watson with the idea because he had  now  turned and he was totally opposed to any  kind of regulation  right whereas he'd been a signer of the  original letter  um they said we'll close down  people could close down cold spring  harbor if there's any danger or anything  that happens  and then people's own personal risks  were beginning to  played on and eventually i think  everybody says we cannot walk out of  here  tomorrow morning saying we don't have  any advice  right so that much especially with the  press there because they were going to  cover it right and that night  i maxine and dave and sydney we drafted  a set of guidelines and recommended that  the nih  use this as a as a sort of outline for a  set of guidelines that would essentially  determine the conditions under which  this research could be done and on the  conditions of which  should not be done and so the whole  thing that came out was very positive we  debated it  and voted on segment by segment until  when  noon came when we had to leave we had an  approved  list of regular was it unanimous or was  it no no there were  i would say it was 95 that's pretty good  pretty amazing going forward even though  the language was  muddled and things like that but we all  agree that we would  smooth things out so i worked on the  language  and dave did and maxine and we finally  got  a document which is now recorded as a  famous document  out of the cilamar conference we look  back on it would say you know we  a bunch of amateurs yeah we mumbled  through a bunch of stuff we made some  decisions  that were smart in retrospect for  example one of the things we did not do  and did not include in anywhere's agenda  was the ethics  we didn't talk about right genetic  testing we didn't talk  about that right we talked about real  experiments  and what the impact of those experiments  would be in the field so the agriculture  nitrogen fixation you know people had  all kinds of ideas  of what we call the low-hanging fruit  what was the thing the infamous  low-lagging treatments  yeah right all of those things were  obvious in retrospect when you look back  you say  most of what's happened nobody even  could conceive about  plus nine yeah and so you know  that's the way science goes you make a  slight breakthrough you got a lot of  very smart people out there very smart  people figure out very quickly how to  use  this new notion new tool or whatever  and the field explodes and that's what's  happened yeah  and why recombinant dna is fantastic so  when  when did the congress get into the act  when did the politicians decide that  this was something they should worry  about  okay so the the congress the  sulmar meeting was held in february of  1975  uh the rest of that year there were  small groups that were meeting to devise  the  guidelines the guidelines came out in  the summer of 76  and that ruled today and then  sniping occurred and the sniping  occurred from different people  who said well these guidelines are not  rigorous enough or we don't trust the  scientists to abide by them  congress began to respond to these  little  barbs and they said on a course that  they were actually going to prohibit  recombinant dna  research in the country and i remember  spending a lot of time in congress and i  can remember  vividly one senator from arkansas  who's normally a liberal and was a  pretty good guy getting up  and saying on the floor of the senate  i never got through high school  chemistry and i don't profess to  understand any of this science  but i believe this is the most dangerous  work ever undertaken  in this country and i would recommend  prohibiting it there were a lot of  people who shared that view  and i am cynical enough to now realize  that what changed the whole picture yeah  and obliterated the concerns  was the founding of genentech and the  first days  stock issue ah money suddenly  it became clear that this was going to  be a commercial  right energy and important and to put  any prohibitions on it was like  shooting yourself in the foot and the  whole thing disappeared so the promise  of making insulin for kids was not the  winning message you think or it was a  message but when you actually saw  something happen  yes that was no longer virtual i believe  that was the thing that actually  interesting was a switch and the  atmosphere  changed completely as soon as it became  a couple of companies  starting up to take advantage of this  technology to do this that and the other  thing  so ultimately the resolution of this was  if i understand if i remember correctly  no law was ever passed in the congress  no law was ever passed  and it was all done by scientists  driving  the development of good regulation  through the funding bodies which then  spread  exactly amazing it's a fantastic example

